  <h2>Why material quality is where most projects go wrong</h2>

  <p>You can't see bad concrete once the shuttering comes off. You can't tell if the waterproofing membrane was applied at half the specified thickness. You can't spot cheap TMT steel from the outside after plastering. This is the uncomfortable truth about residential construction: the decisions that cost you the most happen when no one is watching.</p>

  <p>VP Infra uses AAC (Autoclaved Aerated Concrete) blocks instead of conventional red clay bricks across all its residential projects. The difference matters. AAC blocks reduce the dead load on the structure by nearly 40%, improve thermal insulation by a measurable margin, and lower the building's overall carbon footprint. For the homeowner, this translates into lower summer electricity bills and a structure that performs better in Lucknow's Seismic Zone III conditions.</p>

  <p>For waterproofing, VP Infra's approach is layered. Crystalline compounds go into below-grade and basement areas. Elastomeric membranes cover wet areas like bathrooms and balconies. Polyurethane coatings are applied to exposed terraces. None of this is done as an afterthought. It's part of the grey structure stage, before plaster ever goes up.</p>

